llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
133): the one-token DeltaNet recurrence (gated_delta_net_cuda at head size
128) gives each wave four columns of the state instead of one. The results
are the same bits.

WHY
---
At decode the kernel reads and writes each head's 64 KiB fp32 state once.
For Qwen3.6 35B-A3B that is 2 MiB each way per layer for 32 heads, over 30
DeltaNet layers a token, and the call took 34.6 us: about 120 GB/s. With
one column per wave a lane holds two state values, so a wave keeps two
loads in flight and then waits out two wave-wide reductions in a row. With
four columns the waits of the four columns overlap: 23.4 us, about
180 GB/s. Two columns take 27.0 us; eight take 22.1 us, with half the waves
again.

WHAT
----
gated_delta_net_cuda takes the number of columns per wave as a template
parameter (default 1, so the other head sizes are unchanged); the launcher
passes 4 for head size 128 and shrinks the grid to match. Each column's
arithmetic and reduction order are unchanged, so the attention output and
the state are the same bits.

Result on an RX 590 (Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, experts in system RAM, q8_0
KV cache, flash attention, rocprof):

    gated_delta_net_cuda<128, ...> per call     34.6 -> 23.4 us
    per token (30 calls)                        1.04 -> 0.70 ms
    logits, llama-perplexity over 1,024 tokens as one-token decodes and as
    512-token batches; Qwen3.5 9B decodes       byte-identical

Verified on an RX 590: test-backend-ops test -b ROCm0 passes all 16,460
cases with this patch and 133.

To reproduce, run the llama-perplexity identity check of patch 133 with
and without this patch, and rocprof --hip-trace --stats around llama-bench
-p 0 -n 32 (GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_GRAPHS=1) for the per-call times.

